A||N||33||bHooper||Wiggle in the back end||||2||
Z||000000||bHooper||12-18-2000||09:54 PM||||i just rebuilt/replaced almost everything in the suspension, on my '86 GT.  i used KYB's and Eibach springs.<P>the only things i didn't replace are the rear inner tie rods, the engine mounts and the rear trany mount and tires.<P>sometimes, going down the express way, i get a bit of wiggle in the rear.  it seems to do this on it's on.<P>b4 the rebuild, it would occasionally do this... but it is more profound, now.<P>any thoughts?  is this actually bump steer that i'm feeling?  perhaps more twitchy now that i have the car dialed in at -1 degree of camber all the way?<P>other than that, the car handles like a dream!  i can do a complete 3 point turn in one motion... in the middle of the road!<P>thanks!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||2
Z||000001||Xtreme Fiero's||12-18-2000||10:49 PM||||What are yor Tire Pressures? add a pound or two You will be amazed at he results. What is the Toe ste to it may be in or out to far.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick's Dad||12-18-2000||11:34 PM||||From the title, I thought a pic of a girl from behind would greet me....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Could a weight have fallen from your rim? The "wiggle" you describe, since it's only at highway speeds, sounds like one of your rear tires is out of balance.<P>Also, a "three point turn" is so named because you actually stop twice to reverse motion (Fwd-Rvs-Fwd). What you describe seems to be a "Zero point turn" (not to be confused with a "no point turn," which can be a zero point turn, but does not have to be), which can be a whole lot of fun if the local constabulary does not witness it.<P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||12-19-2000||02:38 AM||||What kind of tires are you running? When I lowered mine I noticed high sidewalls tended to cause that. Also how are your cradle and control arm bushings? The bushings would be more so if it did it under acceleration or deceleration. The rear tierods could be a problem or even a bad bearing/hub assembly.<BR>When I did mine I replaced everything except cradle bushings.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||PlasticWrapd||12-19-2000||06:29 AM||||I had the same problem.  I noticed it more when accelerating in second gear.  Turned out to be the front transmission mount.  I propped the rear decklid open and watched while I drove on a quiet stretch of road.  The engine would lift up off the broken mount and bob up and down.  Relaced the mount and all the "wiggle" went away.  Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||12-19-2000||06:41 AM||||Bump Steer only happens when the srear suspension is compressed.  If your wiggle happens when traveling down the smooth road it is unlikely that bump steer is the culprit.  Have you had a four wheel alignment lately?  I little bit of out of alignment can cause strange things to happen.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||12-19-2000||07:20 AM||||yes...<BR>new cradle bushings...<BR>new control arm bushings...<BR>new outer tie rods...<BR>new hub/bearings...<BR>new ball joints...<BR>old rear inner tie rods... <BR>old (not anchient) rear tires...<P>the tire sidewall thing sounds plausable, also the tie rod thing and the motor mounts are what i was thinking.<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||2
Z||000007||Richard Parnell||12-19-2000||01:08 PM||||Plasticwrapd is correct.  It's your tranny mounts.  I had the same symptoms with the 4 banger in my SC.  It would wiggle.  It was a strange sensation to say the least.||||reg||1
