Z||000011||william_thorniii||12-28-2000||11:54 AM||||hoop,<P>Doh!  Why didn't I remember you had just put a rear sway on!<P>What diameter differential between the front and rear bars is there on your ride?  If one is to simply add a rear bar of similar (or gasp... same... or double gasp larger) diameter as the front, then yes... you could possibly create an oversteer condition.  This is why Pontiac placed the front bar on 84-87 Fiero's... understeer is much safer.  <P>If this is the case all is not lost.  You simply need to either replace the rear bar with one that is less stiff than the front bar, or you need to increase the effective stiffness of the front bar.  There are several ways to do this... theorhetically. <P>Essentially what you need to do is increase the resistance of the front bar to force input from the LCA's.  In other words you need to reduce the moment arm of the front bar, which will in turn reduce the torque input to the arm (Torque=force*distance).  You cannot change the input force so you must change the distance at which it acts.  <P>There are several methods that I am experimenting with now that will help accomplish this.   You could employ threaded rod end-links as Will suggested in an earlier post.  The tunability function could be used to create preload on the bar.  This could conceiveably increase the effective stiffness of the bar.  <P>Another method would be to drill new holes in the LCA to mount the end-link closer to the centerline of the sway bar.  This will quite simply reduce the distance to the centerline of the bar.  This will change the angle of the bar and I have not checked if it will be possible with our geometry.  <P>Another way would be to reduce the compliance in the end links.  You could eliminate bushings in the front and have poly in the back, or have poly in front and rubber in the back or another combination of the three.  <P>The calculations are unbelieveable in the different stiffness between each mm of large diameter bars.  Our stock bars are 23mm in the front.  If one were to go with a 24mm bar in the rear, the rear bar is in excess of 100% more stiff than the front bar.  I should be able to dig up the calculations if you should so desire.  <P>Hope this helps,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000012||bHooper||12-28-2000||12:59 PM||||WTIII.. numbers, numbers, numbers.  But thanks!  I think you helped to explain things, some.  But wouldn't the different size swaybars just contribute to the car kicking out in the turns?  It shouldn't cause the "wiggle" on the straights.<P>The rear swaybar is FOCOA, I don't remember what size it is.  It had been on my car, in the past.  But I removed it when I did the tranny transplant last spring.  <P>I did change the rear endlink, to a poly bushing kit that I bought from Autozone.<P>The front swaybar is factory.  It has a similar bushing/end link kit as the rear. I have not changed the frame bushings, on the front.<P>Thanks!<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000013||william_thorniii||12-28-2000||01:08 PM||||hoop,<P>An oversteer condition can cause the rear to be twitchy even in straights.  This type of problem is more commonly associated with excessive camber in the rear... but I know that you just got back from the alignment shop.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000014||william_thorniii||12-28-2000||01:13 PM||||hoop,<P>Check out <A HREF="http://www.grassrootsmotorsports.com/antirollbars.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.grassrootsmotorsports.com/antirollbars.html</A> <P>This should explain what I was talking about better than I could.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
