Z||000016||bHooper||01-03-2001||09:19 AM||||i removed the rear sway bar, last night... and as far as i can tell, the car seems to be a lot more stable.<P>there is still a slight wiggle, which i am attributing to the rear tires.<P>my conclusion, is the tires sidewalls are weak, therefore the swaybar was magnifying the issue.<P>for the time being, i will leave the rear bar off, until i can afford new tires/rims and a larger front swaybar.<P>i think the rear bar will serve me best on the autocross track, where speeds aren't all that high (for extended periods of time), and oversteer is preferable.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000017||bHooper||01-11-2001||11:24 AM||||so here i sit, reading this strut tower brace thread, when i say to myself: "myself, could this wiggle be attributed to the struts themselves?", "possibly... it could be that they aren't torqued down correctly, at the top, where the mount to the car.  wouldn't any movement, here, be felt as a wiggle in a straight line and unnoticable in a curve or turn?"<P>myself, might be correct.  or not.  but either way, i am going to check into this tonight.<P>question to the forum:  does the alignment tech adjust the struts at the top (i.e.: where they mount to the body?)?<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000018||Oreif||01-11-2001||11:38 AM||||No they don't touch the top of the struts. They adjust it where the axle assembly meets the bottom of the strut. Those 2 big bolts. I guess if the strut tops were loose that could cause some problems.||||reg||1
Z||000019||bHooper||01-11-2001||11:54 AM||||thats exactly what i was thinking.  i did my best to torque everything down, when i reassembled it, however things do settle, or need adjustment after a bit of use.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000020||william_thorniii||01-11-2001||12:24 PM||||hoop,<P>Highly unlikely, but possible.<P>The major disadvantage of McPherson struts is their tendency to bend the strut rod.  If they flex enough it is possible to bind them on the non-linear rod.  If it were transferring significant enough force to elongate the holes in the tower, the strut would have to be bound up for quite a while and seeing much more serious loading than would occur on the road.  Not to say it isn't possible... but it is definitely far to the nearly impossible spectrum.  <P>[edited because I am an idiot] <P>William T. Thorn, III<P><p>[This message has been edited by william_thorniii (edited 01-11-2001).]||||reg||3
