Z||000031||william_thorniii||01-12-2001||04:07 PM||||hoop,<P>It is all about relative stiffness.  The end that is more stiff will take more load.  This is what determines both static and dynamic balance.  Assuming similar upgrades have been done to the front, it would make sense that the front would still be more rigid than the rear.  If not then it is not explainable.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000032||bHooper||01-12-2001||04:59 PM||||the only thing that hasn't changed in the front, is the swaybar.  i am using the factory swaybar and frame bushings, with poly end links.<P>in the rear, the only orginal parts are the inner tie rods, and half shafts (if you want to consider that as suspension related (i would)).<P>after completing the rebuild, the car exhibited an extreme oversteer situation.  now much of this could have been due to the poor mounts, as well as the improper caster settings (deformed poly bushing).  i have corrected the front, and rear.  <P>i think the next step is to reinstall the rear swaybar, and see how the car performs.  obviously, it should be different than it was with the poor tranny/engine mount.<BR>||||reg||1
